#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Which provider and model answered the facts review, as the review agent's tool reports them, and whether that is
the provider and model the review asked for.

  tools/oft/ci/facts-review-served run <directory> <provider>/<model> -- <the agent's command...>
  tools/oft/ci/facts-review-served check < <directory>/served
  tools/oft/ci/facts-review-served asked <provider>/<model>

run first reads where the tool will send its requests (endpoint, below). When that is not the API of the provider
asked for, it does not start the agent. Otherwise it runs the agent's command, whose standard output is its event
stream (`--output-format stream-json --verbose`), in its own environment, which is the agent's (facts-review-run
starts it under `env -i`), and writes into <directory>, beside the comment:
  review.jsonl  the stream, each line as it comes
  review.json   the stream's last `result` event, as it came: the object `--output-format json` writes; empty when
                there is none
  served        the record that facts-review.yml's comment job reads: the line `asked <provider>/<model>`; the line
                `endpoint <where>`, where the tool sends its requests; a line `usage <provider>/<model>` for each entry
                of the result's `.modelUsage` (its `provider`, and its `canonicalModel` or else its key); then one line
                `<provider>/<model> <turns>` for each provider and model that answered, a turn being one model response
                (one message id); `none` where the tool named none. It is written also when run is stopped.
It reads each event as it comes and stops the agent at the first model response whose model is not the model asked
for (or a dated snapshot of it, <model>-YYYYMMDD), or that names no model, and at a line of the stream that is not a
JSON object, rather than let it spend more turns on a review that will not be posted. It exits with the agent's own
status when the agent failed by itself, 4 when it did not start or stopped the agent or the record does not show the
provider and model asked for (the reason on standard error), and 0 otherwise.

check reads a record on standard input and prints one line. It exits 0, with the providers and models that answered
and their turns, when the tool sent its requests to the provider's own API, the provider and model asked for answered
every turn, and every `.modelUsage` entry names them too; 1, with why the review is not to be posted and the counts,
when the endpoint, a provider or a model it names is another; 3, with why, when nothing it names is another but the
review is not attributable: no endpoint or no turn recorded, a provider or model that the tool did not name, or a
record it cannot read.

asked exits 0 when run can check <provider>/<model>, and 2, saying why, when it cannot.

What the tool reports, measured on its version 2.1.310, the one facts-review.yml installs:
- each `assistant` event of the stream carries `.message.model` and `.message.id` from the API's message_start (the
  tool builds the event from it); a response of two content blocks is two events with one id. A message the tool
  writes itself (an API error's text, for one) is an `assistant` event whose model is "<synthetic>" and which the tool
  itself marks `"is_api_error_message": true`: no model answered it, so it is no turn. The model's name alone comes
  from the response, so "<synthetic>" without that mark is a turn, of another model. The model of every event is
  checked, not only the first of a message id.
- the API can hand a response to another model part way (a server fallback). It marks that with a content block
  `{"type": "fallback", "from": {"model": ...}, "to": {"model": ...}}`; the tool renames the response's model to the
  new one only when it asked for the fallback itself, so the block's `to.model` is checked too. Variables that could
  ask the API for one outside the tool (CLAUDE_CODE_EXTRA_BODY, ANTHROPIC_BETAS) are refused, as is the advisorModel
  setting (a server-side tool that another model answers), and a managed policy helper (policyHelper, policyHelpers),
  which gives the tool settings, an `env` among them, another way than a settings file's `env`.
- no event before the last names the provider: `system`/`init` names the model asked for (`.model`) and the key's
  source, and an `assistant` event names no provider. So a turn's model is checked as it comes, and the provider at
  the end.
- the `result` event's `.modelUsage` is keyed by the model the tool asked for, not the one that answered, and each
  entry's `provider` is the tool's API provider, set from its own configuration ("firstParty" for Anthropic's own API;
  its schema lists "bedrock", "vertex", "foundry", "anthropicAws", "mantle" and "gateway" besides). `.modelUsage` is
  {} when no request got a response (a refused key, a provider with no credentials): that names no provider.
- "firstParty" does not say where the requests went: the tool reports it also when ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL names a proxy
  (its own policy code calls that a custom endpoint), which could answer with any model and name it as it likes. So
  the record names the endpoint, and run does not start the agent at another. Of the tool's own variables
  (ANTHROPIC_*, CLAUDE_CODE_*), in its environment or in any `env` it reads, it allows only the credentials and the
  knobs of TOOL_VARIABLES: any other is refused, since several send its requests elsewhere (a base URL, a socket, a
  gateway, a host's credentials file, a settings source this script does not read); an `env` it reads that sets HOME
  or XDG_CONFIG_HOME, which moves the Anthropic profile, is refused too. It reads the settings files a
  machine or a user can write, and the global config in each of the files the tool can take it from (.config.json in
  the config home, and .claude.json and its -staging-oauth, -local-oauth and -custom-oauth names), whose `env` the
  tool also applies as it starts, and the managed settings of the platform (/etc/claude-code, or /Library/Application
  Support/ClaudeCode on macOS) and, under WSL, Windows' (/mnt/c/Program Files/ClaudeCode, and the ClaudeCode policy
  keys of the Windows registry). Remote managed settings that the organization's admin signs (the tool applies routing
  names only from a verified copy) are not read. It runs on Linux (WSL too) and macOS only: elsewhere the tool's
  folders are others, and run refuses.
"""
import collections
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import urllib.parse

# The providers a review may ask for, each with the name the tool gives it in `.modelUsage[*].provider` and the host
# of its own API. An allow list: a provider the tool names otherwise, a new one included, is never taken for the one
# asked for.
TOOL_PROVIDER = {"anthropic": ("firstParty", "api.anthropic.com")}
# The tool's own variables (ANTHROPIC_*, CLAUDE_CODE_*, _CLAUDE_CODE_*), in its environment or a settings file's `env`:
# only these may be set, besides an ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL of the provider's own API. Each names a credential, or a knob
# that moves no request and picks no model. An allow list: the tool reads well over a hundred such names, and several
# (a base URL, a socket, a gateway, a host's credentials file, request fields, headers, settings sources) send its
# requests elsewhere or let another model answer; a name not on this list, a new one included, is refused.
TOOL_VARIABLES = ("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY", "ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN", "ANTHROPIC_IDENTITY_TOKEN",
                  "ANTHROPIC_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE", "ANTHROPIC_FEDERATION_RULE_ID", "ANTHROPIC_ORGANIZATION_ID",
                  "ANTHROPIC_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_ID", "ANTHROPIC_WORKSPACE_ID", "ANTHROPIC_SCOPE",
                  "CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN", "CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TELEMETRY", "CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS")
TOOL_PREFIXES = ("ANTHROPIC_", "CLAUDE_CODE_", "_CLAUDE_CODE_")
# Other variables that let another party answer as the provider's API: the TLS settings that would let a proxy do it.
ELSEWHERE = ("NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED", "NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS", "SSL_CERT_FILE", "SSL_CERT_DIR")
# Variables that move the folder of the Anthropic profile the tool reads, when an `env` it applies as it starts (a
# global config's) sets them: the profile's base_url is then one profile_base_url does not read. In any `env` but the
# process environment, which profile_base_url follows, they are refused.
PROFILE_PLACES = ("HOME", "XDG_CONFIG_HOME")
# The names the tool gives its global config, .claude<name>.json (its own list, 2.1.310).
GLOBAL_CONFIG_NAMES = ("", "-staging-oauth", "-local-oauth", "-custom-oauth")
# The folders of the tool's managed settings (managed-settings.json, managed-settings.d/*.json): the platform's, and
# under WSL also Windows', whose registry keys (SOFTWARE\Policies\ClaudeCode, value Settings) it reads through REG.
MANAGED = {"darwin": "/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode", "linux": "/etc/claude-code",
           "wsl": "/mnt/c/Program Files/ClaudeCode"}
REG = "/mnt/c/Windows/System32/reg.exe"
REG_TIMEOUT = 10   # seconds for one reg.exe query; one that takes longer refuses
PLATFORM = sys.platform
PROXIES = ("HTTPS_PROXY", "HTTP_PROXY", "ALL_PROXY")   # in any case
# Settings, outside `env`, that let another model answer part of a review, or give the tool an `env` another way.
OTHER_MODEL_SETTINGS = ("advisorModel", "policyHelper", "policyHelpers")
STOPPED = 4         # run: the agent was not started or was stopped, or the record does not show the provider asked for
UNATTRIBUTED = 3    # check: no other provider or model answered, but a turn names none, or no turn answered
NAME = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9._:@+\[\]_-]{1,100}")
SYNTHETIC = "<synthetic>"   # the model of a message the tool writes itself


def clean(value):
    """A provider's or model's name as the record writes it: `none` when there is none, and no character outside
    NAME's, since the comment shows it."""
    if not isinstance(value, str) or not value:
        return "none"
    return value if NAME.fullmatch(value) else (re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9._:@+\[\]_-]", "?", value)[:100] or "none")


def under_wsl():
    """Whether this runs under WSL, as the tool tells it: WSL_DISTRO_NAME, or a kernel that names Microsoft or WSL."""
    try:
        with open("/proc/version") as f:
            return bool(os.environ.get("WSL_DISTRO_NAME")) or any(w in f.read().lower() for w in ("microsoft", "wsl"))
    except OSError:
        return bool(os.environ.get("WSL_DISTRO_NAME"))


def registry_settings():
    """The settings of the Windows registry's ClaudeCode policy keys, as JSON texts, under WSL; raises ValueError for
    one it cannot read."""
    texts = []
    for key in ("HKLM", "HKCU") if os.path.exists(REG) else ():
        try:
            answer = subprocess.run([REG, "query", fr"{key}\SOFTWARE\Policies\ClaudeCode", "/v", "Settings"],
                                    capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=REG_TIMEOUT, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
            raise ValueError(f"reg.exe: {type(e).__name__}") from e
        if answer.returncode == 1:   # no such key or value
            continue
        # The tool's own pattern (2.1.310): the value runs from its line to the end of the output.
        value = re.search(r"^[ \t]+Settings[ \t]+REG_(?:EXPAND_)?SZ[ \t]+([\s\S]*)", answer.stdout, re.M | re.I)
        if answer.returncode != 0 or value is None:
            raise ValueError("reg.exe gave no Settings value")
        texts.append(value.group(1).rstrip())
    return texts


def add_settings(sources, settings, unreadable="unreadable-settings"):
    """Adds a settings object's `env`, and its OTHER_MODEL_SETTINGS keys, to `sources`: False when it is no object. An
    `env` that is no object is named `unreadable`."""
    if not isinstance(settings, dict):
        return False
    env = settings.get("env", {})
    sources.append(env if isinstance(env, dict) else {unreadable: ""})
    sources.append({name: "" for name in OTHER_MODEL_SETTINGS if name in settings})
    return True


def asked_for(asked):
    """(provider, model, the tool's name for the provider, the provider's host) of `<provider>/<model>`."""
    provider, _, model = asked.partition("/")
    if provider not in TOOL_PROVIDER or not NAME.fullmatch(model) or model == "none":
        raise ValueError(f"asked for {clean(asked)}: not <provider>/<model> with a provider of "
                         f"{', '.join(sorted(TOOL_PROVIDER))}")
    return (provider, model) + TOOL_PROVIDER[provider]


def is_model(name, model):
    """Whether `name` is the model `model`: the same id, or a dated snapshot of it (`<model>-YYYYMMDD`), the name the
    API answers with for some aliases."""
    return name == model or re.fullmatch(re.escape(model) + r"-[0-9]{8}", name) is not None


def base_url(value, host):
    """None when the URL `value` is https://<host> (port 443 at most), else how the record names it:
    `<scheme>:<host>[:<port>]`, and `+userinfo` when it holds a user or a password, which the record never shows."""
    try:
        url = urllib.parse.urlsplit(str(value))
        port = url.port
    except ValueError:   # a URL the parser refuses, or a port that is not a number
        return "unreadable-url"
    if url.scheme == "https" and url.hostname == host and port in (None, 443) and "@" not in url.netloc:
        return None
    named = f"{url.scheme or 'none'}:{url.hostname or 'none'}{f':{port}' if port is not None else ''}"
    return clean(named + ("+userinfo" if "@" in url.netloc else ""))


def profile_base_url():
    """The `base_url` of the Anthropic profile the tool reads, from <config dir>/configs/<profile>.json: the config dir
    is ANTHROPIC_CONFIG_DIR, else $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/anthropic, else ~/.config/anthropic; the profile is
    ANTHROPIC_PROFILE, else the one active_config names, else `default`. None when there is none;
    `unreadable-profile` when the profile cannot be read."""
    env = os.environ
    directory = env.get("ANTHROPIC_CONFIG_DIR", "").strip() or (
        os.path.join(env["XDG_CONFIG_HOME"].strip(), "anthropic") if env.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", "").strip()
        else os.path.join(env.get("HOME", ""), ".config", "anthropic"))
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(directory, "active_config")) as f:
            active = f.read().strip()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        active = ""
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return "unreadable-profile"
    name = env.get("ANTHROPIC_PROFILE", "").strip() or active or "default"
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(directory, "configs", f"{name}.json")) as f:
            profile = json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except (OSError, ValueError, RecursionError):
        return "unreadable-profile"
    if not isinstance(profile, dict):
        return "unreadable-profile"
    url = profile.get("base_url")
    return url if isinstance(url, str) and url.strip() else None


def endpoint(host):
    """Where the tool will send its requests: `host` when nothing sends them elsewhere, else what does, `+`-joined: an
    ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL, or a profile's base_url, other than https://<host> (as base_url names it), or the name of
    another variable or setting that could send them elsewhere or let another model answer. It reads the agent's
    environment, which is this process's, the `env` of each of the tool's settings files and of its global config,
    which the tool applies as it starts, and the Anthropic profile the tool reads; one it cannot read is
    `unreadable-settings` or `unreadable-profile`. The global config is whichever of these the tool takes, so all are
    read: .config.json in the config home (CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, else ~/.claude), and .claude<s>.json, for each <s> of
    GLOBAL_CONFIG_NAMES, in CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, else the home directory. An empty file holds no `env`. Managed settings
    are read from the platform's folder (MANAGED), and under WSL from Windows' folder and registry keys too: one it
    cannot read is `unreadable-settings` or `unreadable-registry`. On a platform other than Linux or macOS it is
    `unsupported-platform`."""
    sources = [os.environ]
    home = os.environ.get("HOME", "")
    config = [os.path.join(home, ".claude"), os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR")]
    globals_ = [os.path.join(d, ".config.json") for d in config if d] + \
        [os.path.join(d, f".claude{name}.json") for d in (home, config[1]) if d for name in GLOBAL_CONFIG_NAMES]
    if PLATFORM not in ("darwin", "linux"):
        # Elsewhere (Windows, Cygwin, MSYS) the tool's folders are others than these: nothing here shows them.
        return "unsupported-platform"
    wsl = PLATFORM == "linux" and under_wsl()
    managed = [MANAGED[PLATFORM]] + [MANAGED["wsl"]] * wsl
    managed_files = [os.path.join(d, "managed-settings.json") for d in managed] + \
        [p for d in managed for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(glob.escape(d), "managed-settings.d", "*.json")))]
    for path in [os.path.join(d, "settings.json") for d in config if d] + globals_ + [".claude/settings.json",
                 ".claude/settings.local.json"] + managed_files:
        try:
            if path in globals_ and os.path.getsize(path) == 0:
                continue
            with open(path) as f:
                settings = json.load(f)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue
        except (OSError, ValueError, RecursionError):
            return "unreadable-settings"
        if not add_settings(sources, settings):
            return "unreadable-settings"
    try:
        for text in registry_settings() if wsl else ():
            if not add_settings(sources, json.loads(text), "unreadable-registry"):
                return "unreadable-registry"
    except (ValueError, RecursionError):
        return "unreadable-registry"
    found = set()
    profile = profile_base_url()
    if profile == "unreadable-profile":
        found.add(profile)
    elif profile is not None:
        found.add(base_url(profile, host) or host)
    for number, env in enumerate(sources):   # sources[0] is the process environment
        for name, value in env.items():
            if name == "ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL":
                found.add(base_url(value, host) or host)
            elif name.startswith(TOOL_PREFIXES) and name not in TOOL_VARIABLES or name in ELSEWHERE \
                    or name.upper() in PROXIES or name in OTHER_MODEL_SETTINGS \
                    or name in ("unreadable-settings", "unreadable-registry") \
                    or number > 0 and name in PROFILE_PLACES:
                found.add(clean(name))
    found.discard(host)
    return "+".join(sorted(found)) or host


def turns(n):
    return f"{n} turn" if n == 1 else f"{n} turns"


def check(record):
    """(exit status, line) for a record: 0 and the counts when the provider and model asked for answered every turn."""
    asked, where, used, answered = None, [], [], []
    for line in record.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("asked "):
            asked = line[len("asked "):]
        elif line.startswith("endpoint "):
            where.append(line[len("endpoint "):])
        elif line.startswith("usage ") and "/" in line:
            used.append(line[len("usage "):].split("/", 1))
        elif line.strip():
            served, _, n = line.rpartition(" ")
            if "/" not in served or not (n.isascii() and n.isdigit()) or int(n) == 0:
                return UNATTRIBUTED, f"a line of the record of what answered the review is not one: {clean(line)}"
            answered.append((served.split("/", 1), int(n)))
    if asked is None:
        return UNATTRIBUTED, "nothing recorded which provider answered the review: it is not attributable"
    try:
        _, model, want, host = asked_for(asked)
    except ValueError as e:
        return UNATTRIBUTED, str(e)
    counts = ", ".join(f"{provider}/{name} ({turns(n)})" for (provider, name), n in answered)
    usage = ", ".join(f"{provider}/{name}" for provider, name in used) or "nothing"
    if "unsupported-platform" in where:
        return 1, f"it asked for {asked}, but the review ran on a platform where nothing shows where the tool " \
                  "reads its settings"
    if any(w != host for w in where):
        return 1, f"it asked for {asked}, whose API is {host}, but the tool's environment or settings set " \
                  f"{', '.join(clean(w) for w in where)}, which can send its requests elsewhere or let another model " \
                  f"answer, and {counts or 'no turn'} answered"
    if any(provider not in ("none", want) or name != "none" and not is_model(name, model)
           for (provider, name) in used + [served for served, _ in answered]):
        return 1, f"it asked for {asked}, which the tool calls {want}/{model}, but {counts or 'no turn'} " \
                  f"answered, and the tool's usage names {usage}"
    if not where:
        return UNATTRIBUTED, f"it asked for {asked}, and nothing recorded where the tool sent its requests, though " \
                             f"{counts or 'no turn'} answered: it is not attributable"
    if not answered:
        return UNATTRIBUTED, f"it asked for {asked}, and no turn named the provider and model that answered it: it " \
                             "is not attributable"
    if any("none" in (provider, name) for (provider, name), _ in answered):
        return UNATTRIBUTED, f"it asked for {asked}, and {counts} answered, where none is a provider or model the " \
                             "tool did not name: it is not attributable"
    if not used or any("none" in (provider, name) for provider, name in used):
        return UNATTRIBUTED, f"it asked for {asked}, and {counts} answered, but the tool's usage names {usage}, " \
                             "where none is a provider or model the tool did not name: it is not attributable"
    return 0, counts


def stop(agent):
    """Stop the agent and every process it started: it runs in a process group of its own. A second stop of this
    script while it does so (SIGTERM, SIGINT) waits until it is done, so that the SIGKILL is always sent."""
    held = {s: signal.signal(s, signal.SIG_IGN) for s in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGINT)}
    try:
        try:
            os.killpg(agent.pid, signal.SIGTERM)
            agent.wait(timeout=30)
        except (ProcessLookupError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            pass
        try:
            os.killpg(agent.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        except ProcessLookupError:
            pass
        agent.wait()
    finally:
        for s, handler in held.items():
            signal.signal(s, handler)


def write_record(directory, asked, where, seen):
    """Writes `served`, then review.json, from what read has seen so far: the record also when run is stopped."""
    result = seen["result"]
    usage = result.get("modelUsage") if result is not None else None
    # An entry that is not an object names no provider or model: `none`, so the record is not attributable.
    used = sorted({(clean(u.get("provider")), clean(u.get("canonicalModel") or key)) if isinstance(u, dict)
                   else ("none", clean(key)) for key, u in usage.items()}) if isinstance(usage, dict) else []
    provider = "+".join(sorted({provider for provider, _ in used})) or "none"
    counts = collections.Counter(f"{provider}/{model}" for model in seen["answered"].values())
    record = f"asked {asked}\nendpoint {where}\n" + "".join(f"usage {provider}/{model}\n" for provider, model in used) \
        + "".join(f"{served} {n}\n" for served, n in sorted(counts.items()))
    with open(f"{directory}/served", "w") as f:
        f.write(record)
    with open(f"{directory}/review.json", "wb") as f:
        if result is not None:
            f.write(seen["result_line"].rstrip(b"\r\n"))
    return record


def run(directory, asked, command):
    _, model, _, host = asked_for(asked)
    # What read has seen: message id -> the model that answered it, and the last result event, parsed and as it came.
    seen = {"answered": {}, "result": None, "result_line": None}
    stopped, status = None, 0
    for name in ("review.json", "served"):
        if os.path.exists(f"{directory}/{name}"):
            os.remove(f"{directory}/{name}")
    where = endpoint(host)
    try:
        with open(f"{directory}/review.jsonl", "wb") as stream:
            if where == "unsupported-platform":
                stopped = f"did not start the agent: on {clean(PLATFORM)}, this script does not know where the " \
                          "tool reads its settings (it knows Linux, WSL and macOS)"
            elif where != host:
                stopped = f"did not start the agent: the tool's environment or settings set {where}, " \
                          f"which can send its requests elsewhere than {host} or let another model answer"
            else:
                status, stopped = read(command, stream, model, seen)
    finally:
        record = write_record(directory, asked, where, seen)
    if stopped:
        print(f"facts-review-served: {stopped}", file=sys.stderr)
        return STOPPED
    if status != 0:
        return status if status > 0 else 128 - status
    code, line = check(record)
    if code != 0:
        print(f"facts-review-served: {line}", file=sys.stderr)
        return STOPPED
    return 0


def read(command, stream, model, seen):
    """Runs the agent and reads its stream into `stream` and `seen`, stopping it at the first model response that is
    not of `model`, or at a line that is not a JSON object: (the agent's status, why it was stopped)."""
    answered, stopped, agent = seen["answered"], None, None
    try:
        agent = subprocess.Popen(command, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, start_new_session=True)
        for number, line in enumerate(agent.stdout, 1):
            stream.write(line)
            stream.flush()
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            try:
                event = json.loads(line)
            except (ValueError, RecursionError) as e:
                event = e
            if not isinstance(event, dict):
                # A line that hides a turn would be a turn not checked. The tool writes only events, one a line.
                why = "nested too deeply to read" if isinstance(event, RecursionError) else \
                    "not JSON" if isinstance(event, ValueError) else "not a JSON object"
                stopped = f"stopped the agent: line {number} of its stream is {why}: what answered is not attributable"
                seen["result"] = seen["result_line"] = None
                break
            if event.get("type") == "result":
                seen["result"], seen["result_line"] = event, line
            if event.get("type") != "assistant":
                continue
            message = event.get("message") if isinstance(event.get("message"), dict) else {}
            if message.get("model") == SYNTHETIC and event.get("is_api_error_message") is True:
                continue
            key = message.get("id") if isinstance(message.get("id"), str) else f"#{len(answered)}"
            if key not in answered:
                answered[key] = clean(message.get("model"))
            # The response's model, then the model of each server fallback that a content block hands it to; the raw
            # names are compared, not the record's cut ones. Content that is not a list hides what it holds.
            content = message.get("content") if message.get("content") is not None else []
            handed = [block.get("to").get("model") if isinstance(block.get("to"), dict) else None
                      for block in content if isinstance(block, dict) and block.get("type") == "fallback"] \
                if isinstance(content, list) else [None]
            for answer, how in [(message.get("model"), "")] + [(m, " (a server fallback within the response)")
                                                               for m in handed]:
                if isinstance(answer, str) and is_model(answer, model):
                    continue
                answered[key] = name = clean(answer)
                name = "no model it named" if name == "none" else name
                stopped = f"stopped the agent: turn {list(answered).index(key) + 1} was answered by {name}{how}, " \
                          f"not {model}"
                break
            if stopped:
                break
        if stopped:
            stop(agent)
        return agent.wait(), stopped
    except BaseException:
        if agent is not None:
            stop(agent)
        raise
    finally:
        if agent is not None:
            agent.stdout.close()


def main(argv):
    usage = "usage: facts-review-served run <directory> <provider>/<model> -- <command...> | check < <record> | " \
            "asked <provider>/<model>"
    if argv[:1] == ["check"] and len(argv) == 1:
        try:
            code, line = check(sys.stdin.read())
        except Exception as e:   # a record it cannot read is no evidence, and never the exit of another provider, 1
            code, line = UNATTRIBUTED, f"the record of what answered the review could not be read " \
                                       f"({type(e).__name__}): it is not attributable"
        print(line)
        return code
    running = argv[:1] == ["run"] and len(argv) > 4 and argv[3] == "--"
    if running or argv[:1] == ["asked"] and len(argv) == 2:
        try:
            asked_for(argv[2] if running else argv[1])
        except ValueError as e:
            print(f"facts-review-served: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if not running:
            return 0
        # A stop of this script (the step's timeout, a cancelled run) stops the agent too, through read's cleanup, and
        # the record is written of what answered until then.
        signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda *_: sys.exit(128 + signal.SIGTERM))
        try:
            return run(argv[1], argv[2], argv[4:])
        except Exception as e:   # read stopped the agent already
            print(f"facts-review-served: could not record what answered the review ({type(e).__name__}): it is not "
                  "attributable", file=sys.stderr)
            return STOPPED
    print(usage, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
